 "final": "The torsion block spec is complete. It passes validate_spec with no findings, and every item has a source tag (none are tagged assumed).\n\n- **Domain:** a 100 mm cube in mm and N, steel (E = 210,000 N/mm\u00b2, \u03bd = 0.3), volume fraction 0.15. With 1 mm elements that is a 100\u00d7100\u00d7100 grid, 1,000,000 elements.\n- **Support:** the whole bottom face (z = 0) is fixed in x, y and z (10,201 nodes).\n- **Loads:** four 10\u00d710 mm pads on the top face, flush with the midpoint of each edge (121 nodes each). Each pad's centre is 45 mm from the vertical axis. Each force is 18,000 N\u00b7mm \u00f7 (4 \u00d7 45 mm) = 100 N, pointed to give a counter-clockwise torque seen from above:\n  - edge at y = 0, pad centre (50, 5): +x\n  - edge at y = 100, pad centre (50, 95): \u2212x\n  - edge at x = 100, pad centre (95, 50): +y\n  - edge at x = 0, pad centre (5, 50): \u2212y\n\n  The four forces cancel, and together they give exactly +18 N\u00b7m about z.\n- **Render check:** the top-view render shows the load arrows on the top face and the support on the bottom face.\n\nDONE. Assumptions: steel taken as E = 210 GPa, \u03bd = 0.3. Each pad's force is spread evenly over its area, with the lever arm measured to the pad centre (45 mm, giving 100 N per pad rather than 90 N). No keep-in solid regions were added under the pads.",
